A||||7||California Kid||Production Error, Rear Fascia Doesn't  Fit Properly At Left Rear Qtr Pnl Side Marker.||||1||
Z||000000||California Kid||09-01-2001||08:50 AM||||I've noticed quite a few GT's have the subject condition, where the rear fascia upper molding feature doesn't line up the the same feature at the side maker lamp (fascia sets low by about 3-4 mm). It appears GM's expensive master robot machine wasn't set correctly for a very long time, as this condition spans a couple model years and a very high number of vehicles<P>My question is: Have any of you corrected this fit and how did you do it??  Looks like you can just remove the left taillamp, losen fascia bolts and shim it up??? Appears simple to correct, just wondering if it gets more complicated than it appears.<P>AAhhhh.......The minor details of show cars!!! ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Wolfhound||09-01-2001||09:42 AM||||The rear facia attaches to a shaped piece of metal across the top of the rear sub bumper. this piece of metal can be adjusted up and down in slots where it attaches. You must remove the rear facia to do this.<P>I found the correct location by noting a point where it was correct. <BR>I then removed the facia and reinstalled the tail lights. I took a measurment from the top of the metal strip to the bottom of the tail lights at this point and made it consistant across the rear of the car.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pontiaddict||09-01-2001||09:19 PM||||You see it on a lot of cars because the fascias tend to streach at the bolt holes after a few years, causing the whole thing to sag.||||reg||1
Z||000003||California Kid||09-01-2001||10:02 PM||||Thanks for the information Wolfhound!!  Looks like a project for this fall or next spring.  A lot of work for a 3mm mismatch but what the heck, I know it's there, and it bugs me.||||reg||6
Z||000004||fierodave||09-01-2001||11:58 PM||||Just pull the taillight on that side, Remove the large plastic rivets accross the top of the facia and pull the cover out(back) far enough to put a few washers between the facia and that metal pan  it's a 15 minute adjustment.<BR>FD||||reg||1
Z||000005||California Kid||09-02-2001||12:17 AM||||Thanks Fierodave, all input appreciated.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||artherd||09-02-2001||12:28 AM||||I wouldn't exactly try to roll a ball bearing in the groove of ANY Fiero unfortunately :S)<P>I belive you are expierencing some sag in the fascia, ajusting the mounts will cure it for a while, probally indefinately if you don't leave the car in direct sunlight for a great long while (readl: weeks/months)<P>Best!!!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000007||California Kid||09-02-2001||12:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>I belive you are expierencing some sag in the fascia, ajusting the mounts will cure it for a while, probally indefinately if you don't leave the car in direct sunlight for a great long while (readl: weeks/months)<P>Best!!!<BR>Ben.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>This is diffently not a sag condition, absolutlely no distortion in fascia.  Talked with a Quality Person from the old Fiero Plant and he admitted that they didn't catch this problem for a very long time, and it was due to improper setup of the huge machine that set all body panel mount points.<BR>So far I've seen this condition on every GT that I looked at in shows (that haven't been taken apart and put back together by a skilled person).<P>||||reg||1
